0027 — `arc-cap-butt`: an arc whose ends stop on the radius.

An arc was round-capped and nothing else: a half-disc of the stroke's width
on each end. An arc as wide as its radius was therefore a sector with two
blobs on it, and a radar wedge or a pie chart had to be an image.
`arc-cap-butt` ends the band on the two radii through `arcStart` and
`arcStart + arcSweep`; `arc-cap-round` is the default and says so. Both arc
paths — the screen-space one and 0026's transformed one — read it, so a
butt-capped wedge turns with its wrapper like any arc.

What it adds: PROP `arcCap` = 183, the next after 0019's `bgClip`, an enum
`ArcCap { Round: 0, Butt: 1 }` (VALUE_KIND int, not animatable); the
generated `spec.rs`, the microts spec and component types; `Resolved.arc_cap`
in `style.rs`; the two tokens in `tailwind.ts`, a third (`arc-cap-square`)
refused as an unknown utility. Class `upstream`: a declaration the core
turns into RECT runs every backend already draws.

Guard: `a_butt_capped_arc_as_wide_as_its_radius_is_a_pie_sector`
(`engine/core/src/tests.rs`): a 40 px-wide quarter arc of radius 40 is a
quarter disc — area within 1.5 % of pi r^2 / 4 and no ink a pixel into any
other quadrant — unturned and under a 90 degree wrapper; with no cap
declared, and with `arc-cap-round`, it is the round-capped arc. Red with
the cap ignored (area 2,513 against the quarter disc's 1,257), green with
it. `tests/tailwind.test.ts` pins the compiler side.

Ledgers, against the same tree without this patch: no digest and no
`visibility` row moves — no composition declares a cap, and the default is
the round cap every arc had.

`git apply` skips everything above the first `diff --git`.

